Import "../Computing.lingua"

Preserve process state while separating eligibility from scheduling policy (@computing-scheduling-boundary: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Measure-a-memory-hierarchy-under-a-declared-cost-model.lingua#r_008EC6006BF0912737390C8789") { r_009ADB7C7B16D8EF5718E860FA
A scheduler selects among eligible processes. In our model a finite sequence of process identifiers is supplied externally, and each selected ready process performs one complete instruction. This is a controlled schedule for an experiment, not an algorithm that guarantees fairness, bounded response time or eventual completion for arbitrary programs.

Each step selects one saved process record and its table. The other process record, protected tables and program descriptions remain unchanged. A switch need not reset acc or pc: the next process has its own saved values. Nor does the switch copy all physical memory; in the conceptual model that memory remains while address interpretation changes. The host implementation's full-state copying is used only to return inspectable snapshots.

Consider process 0 setting acc=40, storing to virtual 0, then waiting. Process 1 sets acc=90 and stores to its own virtual 0, then reads the shared page. The waiting process must retain acc=40 and its continuation even though process 1's acc changes to 12. Waking process 0 restores eligibility; it does not use process 1's last accumulator as its own. The separate records make this invariant explicit.

Private-memory isolation can make some schedules observationally equivalent, but intentional sharing can expose order. In a variant where both page-2 mappings refer to writable frame 5, a write through process 0's virtual 8 becomes visible through process 1's virtual 8. That is authorized sharing, not a violation of the baseline proof: its disjointness premise was changed. A load before the write can see the old value, while a load after it sees the new value. Atomically copying a range in this model does not solve all higher-level concurrent protocols.

Blocking, failure and an observation budget remain separate. A program waiting for an event that never arrives may never finish even under an otherwise fair scheduler. A finite trace ending early proves only its prefix. A fault explains a stopped process under a specific rule. Richer threads, synchronization, resource deadlocks, scheduling proofs and asynchronous devices require later common/branch formation.

**Work:** derive the saved-state invariant by induction over the selected-process trace. List every assumption needed for the private-memory argument and one schedule-sensitive shared-memory observation. Connect [Derive a loop from an invariant and a stopping rule](Derive-a-loop-from-an-invariant-and-a-stopping-rule.lingua#r_02552612F644E09F0E388FB4B9): a termination proof needs a decreasing measure and progress assumptions; merely alternating process identifiers supplies neither for a blocked program.

